] [17592186151096 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "681042cb-b356-52a1-a7cd-dc87fe9884f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186151338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186151339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186151340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186151341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186151342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151337 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151337 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151337 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e77fee99-c622-54ea-b69b-57b587cfd451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151338 #edb/ref 1080 "48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151338 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151338 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e187515-112c-5fef-8d99-85b05dad354d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151339 #edb/ref 1080 "36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151339 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151339 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a6c5b25f-2aca-5694-a936-b46b473d1f82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151340 #edb/ref 1080 "24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151340 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151340 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aeb60cdb-2cff-5660-9b07-7746ca5105af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151341 #edb/ref 1080 "34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151341 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151341 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f8641e55-a99c-5e75-bfc4-533debe3da92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151342 #edb/ref 1080 "52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151342 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151342 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "219ccc4f-b69e-5451-80c1-f2bb05607196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151957 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151957 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2acbb9f2-ae84-536f-86aa-a1ca9203362f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151957 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1036 &= \\begin{vmatrix}555(1) & +\\end{vmatrix}481 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 555 &= \\end{vmatrix}481(1)\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 74 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 481|= & 74(6)\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}37 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙ \\\\\n74 &= \\begin{vmatrix}37(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}37}$. Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555$ is $37$. Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(1\\,036, 555) = 37\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151993 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151993 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "012fbcd1-3203-5247-8294-32bf795aadaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151993 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n713 &= \\begin{vmatrix}322(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}69 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 322 &= \\end{vmatrix}69(4)\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 46 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 69|= & 46(1)\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}23 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙ \\\\\n46 &= \\begin{vmatrix}23(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}23}$. Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322$ is $23$. Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(713, 322) = 23\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152033 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152033 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7$\n2. $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 1$\n3. $651$ and $574$ are coprime" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6699fbf-a942-5828-ab49-8e961e47d3c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152033 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n$651|=\\begin{vmatrix}574 \\cdot 1 & +\\end{vmatrix}77; ↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 574|=\\end{vmatrix}77 \\cdot 7\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 35 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 77|= & 35 \\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙; 35|=\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\cdot 5 & +\\end{vmatrix}0$\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{blue}7}$. So, the greatest common divisor of $651$ and $574$ is $7$:\n$\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) > 1$, the numbers are not coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152166 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152166 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152166 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Larger Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152166 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152166 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "edfa7501-4de4-5087-80fe-af2a91e53487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152191 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152191 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152191 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152191 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152191 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Two Three-Digit Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152191 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152191 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9625025a-4737-54ac-b218-56909013019a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152223 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether Two Integers are Coprime" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152223 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186152033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152223 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e2cb28a0-b456-55cc-94c3-2a35edde21be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152343 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152343 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152343 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152343 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152343 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152343 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152343 #edb/ref 1045 "The Euclidean Algorithm" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152343 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152343 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152343 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0b186ef3-83ed-56eb-91fa-128e647dbb59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152764 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152764 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77116" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152764 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $296$ and $185$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152764 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b810733-c1e7-54dd-bed6-851e7f2c57d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152805 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152805 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152805 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $1248$ and $816$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152805 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9e7b480-a26a-5ec4-83ee-1874930791b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153068 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153068 #edb/ref 1061 23986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153068 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "202d3dad-ca1f-5d2e-bc14-a95bf2c61c8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153068 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153069 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153069 #edb/ref 1061 23999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153069 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ed62451a-c11b-56f1-b0d3-c0dee548aa07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153069 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153108 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153108 #edb/ref 1061 25398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153108 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8a8084a-cdce-5984-bc42-9aa6ddf44444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153108 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153111 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153111 #edb/ref 1061 25439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153111 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe19cdaa-c33f-5d3c-a3d0-046387e9d786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153111 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153167 #edb/ref 1061 32667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153167 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7dbafcc0-dbac-5cb9-9883-cefb46b45ab2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153167 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153356 #edb/ref 1064 "The **greatest common divisor** (or $\\textrm{gcd}$) of two integers is the largest positive integer that divides both of the integers. For example,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(48,18) = 6\n$$\n\nbecause $6$ is the largest integer that divides both $48$ and $18$.\n\nWe often wish to compute the greatest common divisor of two large numbers. Doing this by inspection is hard. Another method is to use prime factorization, but this is also difficult and inefficient for large numbers.\n\nAnother method, known as the **Euclidian algorithm** is very efficient, despite being over $2\\,000$ years old.\n\nLet's demonstrate how to compute $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$ using the Euclidian algorithm. The first step is to apply the division algorithm and write down\n\n$$\n48 = 18q + r\n$$\n\nWhen we do, we get\n\n$$\n48 = {\\color{blue}{18}}(2) + {\\color{red}12}\n$$\n\nWe ignore the quotient $2$ for now and focus on the ${\\color{blue}{18}}$ (the smaller of our two numbers) and the remainder ${\\color{red}12}$. We apply the division algorithm again to these two numbers, and this gives\n\n$$\n18 = {\\color{blue}{12}}(1) + {\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nApplying the division algorithm to the number ${\\color{blue}{12}}$ and the remainder ${\\color{red}6}$ gives\n\n$$\n12 = {\\color{blue}{6}}(2) + {\\color{red}0}\n$$\n\nWe have now reached a remainder of ${\\color{red}0}$, which means that we are done.\n\nIn practice, we normally write the steps underneath one another, like so.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n48 &= \\begin{vmatrix}18(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}12 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 18 &= \\end{vmatrix}12(1)\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 6 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 12|= & 6(2)\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}6}$. The Euclidean algorithm states that this is the greatest common divisor that we seek. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(48,18) = {\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we note that two integers $a$ and $b$ are **coprime** if\n\n$$\n\\gcd(a,b) = 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153356 #edb/ref 1065 4002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153356 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a4cf99d3-137a-53e9-ab7d-2187836c5156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153356 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153380 #edb/ref 1064 "We now return to the question of why the Euclidean algorithm works. To help us to understand, let's consider once more how to compute $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$. Using the algorithm, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n48 &= \\begin{vmatrix}18(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}12 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 18 &= \\end{vmatrix}12(1)\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 6 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 12|= & 6(2)\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the process gives the following pairs of integers:\n\n$$\n48,18\\quad \\longrightarrow\\quad 18,12 \\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad 12,6\n$$\n\nWe claim that $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$ must be the same as $\\textrm{gcd}(18,12)$. To see why, notice that we used the division algorithm to write\n\n$$\n48 = 18(2) + 12\n$$\n\nIf $48$ and $18$ are divisible by some integer $n$, then $n$ must also divide $18(2)$. This means that $n$ must divide $48-18(2)$, i.e., $n$ must divide $12$. So any number $n$ that divides both $48$ and $18$ must also divide both $18$ and $12$.\n\nRepeating the same argument, we are able to conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(18,12) = \\textrm{gcd}(12,6)\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(12,6) = 6\n$$\n\nwe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(48,18) = \\textrm{gcd}(18,12) = \\textrm{gcd}(12,6) = 6\n$$\n\nIn this example, the algorithm concluded in three steps, but the argument can be extended to many steps." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153380 #edb/ref 1065 4192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153380 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9545489b-ab9a-5913-8dae-c365f08c5a50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153380 #edb/ref 1067 "Why Does the Euclidean Algorithm Work?" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
